    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "One immediate drawback of measuring a QTL effect by its variance in an outbred population is that even a completely linked QTL with a large effect can nonetheless have a small $ \\sigma_{m}^{2} $. Consider a strictly additive diallelic QTL with allele frequency p, where $ \\sigma_{A}^{2} = 2a^{2}p(1 - p) $. Even if a is large, the additive genetic variance can still be quite small if the QTL allele frequencies are near zero or one. An alternative way of visualizing this relationship is to note that the probability of a QTL-informative sire is $ 2p(1 - p) $. If this is small, even if a is large, $ \\sigma_{A}^{2} $ will be small, as most families will not be informative. In those rare informative families, however, the between-marker effect is large. Combining this",
